   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Friday, July 22, 2016 at 3:26:51 AM UTC-4, Jan wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">Is it possible to not partition since the infinite loss functions are all linked quadratically. &quot;x&quot; being continuous instead of discrete.<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span style=\"color:#000\">model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>   </span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\"></span><span style=\"color:#000\"></span><span style=\"color:#660\"></span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> gamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\"></span></code></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> alpha_1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x^2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> alpha_2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> alpha_3</span></code></span></code></span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"></span></code></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> beta_1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x^2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> beta_2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> beta_3</span></code></span></code></span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#000\"></span></code> <br></span></div></code></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>Yes, but see this thread on polynomial functions of X<br><br><a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/39cE2C_S2Fs/discussion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/39cE2C_S2Fs/discussion&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792460731940000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2eUzyMWYjAX2zCwgC3LfsY\">https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/39cE2C_S2Fs/discussion</a> <br><br>Ben<br></div></div>"
    ]
