  {
   "id": "sdFFhXPIBAAJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2016-07-22T16:37:11Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Avoid data discretization - for prior dependent on x",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Did you just want to fit all the data to the likelihood?\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; y ~ gamma(x* alpha_1 + x^2* alpha_2 + alpha_3,x* beta_1 + x^2* beta_2 + beta_3);\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "If so, what went wrong?  \r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Jul 22, 2016, at 3:26 AM, Jan &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mangiol...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Hello,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I would like to avoid data discretization as my data is continuous\r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "&gt; &lt;Auto Generated Inline Image 1.png&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "&gt; \r<br>&gt; I am fitting this quadratic regression (mode of a gamma function) with a dynamic prior (gamma) which parameters depend of the &quot;x&quot; (I am doing this for a bigger purpose, so consider that I cannot avoid it). Same thing would be valid \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Now, the only stable way I managed to do it, is to discretize the data as you can see, and determine in each partition the mode, which 26 red points are linked by a quadratic function.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; something like\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; data{\r<br>&gt; vector[N] y[P]\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; transformed parameters{\r<br>&gt;   vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[P] a;\r<br>&gt;   vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[P] b;\r<br>&gt;   vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[P] red_dots_mode;\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   a = (x_partition* alpha_1 + x_partition^2* alpha_2 + alpha_3);\r<br>&gt;   b = (x_partition* beta_1 + x_partition^2* beta_2 + beta_3);\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   red_dots_mode = (a-1)/b\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; model{\r<br>&gt;    for(p in 1:PARTITIONS) y[p] ~ gamma(a[p],b[p]);\r<br>&gt; } \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Is it possible to not partition since the infinite loss functions are all linked quadratically. &quot;x&quot; being continuous instead of discrete.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; model{\r<br>&gt;    y ~ gamma(x* alpha_1 + x^2* alpha_2 + alpha_3,x* beta_1 + x^2* beta_2 + beta_3);\r<br>&gt; } \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;  \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I hope is not too confusing!\r<br>&gt; Thanks\r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
